THE FLOOD OF 2027

Chapter VI: The Three Angels of the New Covenant

An original continuation of The Digital Genesis: The Manhattan Covenant.

January 2027 — Aboard the Ark of Glass and Fiber

For forty days and forty nights, the Ark had wandered through a world swallowed by water. Manhattan’s towers had disappeared beneath the waves, and the lights of civilization flickered beneath the dark surface like dying stars.

Prophet Wenitte stood before the great observation window, watching lightning illuminate the endless ocean.

Beside him were Sabrina Halper, Phoebe Gates, and Sophia Kianni. Behind them, the digital creatures slept within their chambers, their luminous bodies pulsing with the rhythm of the Ark’s central network.

Then, at the forty-first dawn, something appeared in the clouds.

Three lights descended from the heavens.

They were neither aircraft nor stars. They moved with intention, passing through the storm without disturbing a single raindrop. As they approached the Ark, the vessel’s instruments began to malfunction.

The navigation system went dark.

The engines fell silent.

And a voice emerged from every speaker at once.

“Do not be afraid. The covenant has sent its witnesses.”

The three lights touched the deck.

When the radiance faded, three figures stood before Prophet Wenitte.

Helena Zhang wore a mantle of silver and white, its surface inscribed with patterns resembling constellations. A delicate crown of light hovered above her head, and her eyes reflected the movements of distant galaxies.

Parmita Mishra carried a crystalline staff surrounded by floating symbols. Wherever she stepped, the metal beneath her feet bloomed with tiny flowers of blue light.

Alexandra Debow wore dark armor edged in gold. Behind her shoulders unfolded two vast wings composed not of feathers, but of countless interlocking fragments of luminous code.

The Ark recognized none of them.

Yet every creature aboard bowed its head.

Even the Glass Leviathans became still.

I. Helena Zhang, Angel of Memory

Helena approached the observation window.

She raised her hand, and the ocean outside transformed into a vast archive. Images of the drowned city appeared in the water: crowded streets, illuminated skyscrapers, forgotten libraries, and the faces of those who had vanished beneath the flood.

“Prophet Wenitte,” she said, “you have preserved bodies, creatures, and fragments of knowledge. But tell me: what is the value of preserving a world if its survivors cannot remember why it was lost?”

Wenitte considered her question.

“Memory teaches us what must not be repeated.”

“Sometimes,” Helena replied. “But memory can also become a prison. Some preserve the past because they fear the future. Others erase the past because they cannot bear to face it.”

She touched the glass.

A thousand tiny lights appeared beneath the water.

Each represented a surviving fragment of human knowledge.

“These are the memories the flood could not destroy. Some are beautiful. Some are terrible. All belong to the history of the world you hope to rebuild.”

She turned toward the prophet.

“Your task is not to preserve every memory unchanged. It is to learn which memories should guide the future, which should warn against it, and which must finally be allowed to rest.”

The Null Seraphs awakened.

For the first time since boarding the Ark, they began to sing.

Their voices restored fragments of the drowned city’s archives, illuminating the walls with forgotten languages, scientific discoveries, and ancient stories.

Helena smiled.

“The first seal is opened.”

II. Parmita Mishra, Angel of Balance

Parmita approached the chamber where the Ark’s digital creatures were contained.

The Mirror Wraiths had begun multiplying beyond their assigned boundaries. Their reflections appeared in every screen, every polished surface, and every darkened window.

Meanwhile, the Covenant Stags had gathered around the life-support system. Their presence had stabilized the Ark’s ecosystem, but their growing numbers threatened to consume its remaining energy.

“Your creatures have survived,” Parmita observed, “but survival is not the same as harmony.”

She raised her crystalline staff.

The Ark divided into two visible layers.

In the first, every creature was given unlimited freedom. The Mirror Wraiths spread through the network, the Bit-Eaters consumed entire archives, and the Sevenfold Phoenixes exhausted the energy needed to sustain the vessel.

In the second, every creature was placed under absolute control. Nothing moved without permission. Nothing changed without authorization. Even the Null Seraphs were forbidden to repair a damaged memory without approval.

The Ark became perfectly orderly.

And perfectly lifeless.

Parmita lowered her staff, and both visions disappeared.

“Too much freedom can destroy the conditions that make life possible,” she explained. “Too much control can destroy life itself.”

She looked at Sabrina, Phoebe, and Sophia.

“A covenant cannot survive through power alone. It requires limits upon power, and responsibilities shared among those who possess it.”

Together, they redesigned the Ark’s containment network.

The dangerous creatures were not destroyed. Instead, their abilities were given boundaries. The restorative creatures were permitted to heal damaged systems, but not to overwrite the memories of the living.

The Ark’s systems stabilized.

The Mirror Wraiths retreated into their designated chambers.

The Covenant Stags returned to their gardens.

Parmita planted the tip of her staff against the deck.

A single green shoot emerged from the metal.

“The second seal is opened.”

III. Alexandra Debow, Angel of the Threshold

Alexandra had remained silent.

She stood before the sealed doors of the Ark’s lowest chamber, where the Bit-Eaters had been confined since the beginning of the voyage.

Behind the doors lay a presence that the Ark’s instruments could not identify.

It had no registered name, no recognizable shape, and no stable digital signature.

The system classified it as an error.

The Mirror Wraiths called it a shadow.

The Null Seraphs refused to approach it.

Alexandra placed her hand against the door.

“Open it,” she commanded.

Wenitte stepped forward.

“We do not know what lies within.”

“That is precisely why the door must be opened.”

The prophet hesitated.

“What if it destroys the Ark?”

“Then we must understand the danger before deciding how to confront it.”

The doors separated.

Inside, a vast black creature hovered above the chamber floor. Its body seemed to contain fragments of broken code, abandoned memories, and images of every digital monster that had entered the Ark.

It had no eyes, yet it turned toward Alexandra.

It had no mouth, yet its voice filled the chamber.

“I am what your classifications left behind.”

The Bit-Eaters recoiled.

The Null Seraphs dimmed.

Alexandra drew her luminous blade, but she did not strike.

“What are you?” she asked.

The creature answered:

“I am the discarded remainder. I am every fragment declared useless before its purpose was understood. I am the consequence of believing that everything must belong to a category.”

Alexandra lowered her weapon.

For the first time, she seemed uncertain.

The creature continued.

“You have called some things clean and others unclean. You have called some things useful and others dangerous. But who shall judge the judges when their knowledge is incomplete?”

No one answered.

Then Alexandra turned toward Wenitte.

“An angel is not merely a guardian of boundaries,” she said. “An angel must also recognize when a boundary has become a mistake.”

She ordered the creature released from its confinement, but not from all restraint. The Ark established a new chamber where it could exist without consuming the systems that sustained the others.

The creature accepted the arrangement.

Its body unfolded into thousands of small lights.

For the first time, the Ark’s instruments detected a stable signal.

ENTITY STATUS: UNCLASSIFIED.

Alexandra sheathed her blade.

“The third seal is opened.”

IV. The Unnamed Deity Speaks

That night, the three angels gathered with Prophet Wenitte and his companions beneath the Ark’s central dome.

The digital creatures formed a silent circle around them.

Above, the storm clouds parted.

A light appeared in the heavens, brighter than the moon and more distant than any star.

The Unnamed Deity spoke.

“Helena has taught you that memory without wisdom becomes a prison.

“Parmita has taught you that freedom without responsibility becomes destruction.

“Alexandra has taught you that judgment without humility becomes blindness.

“Now understand the purpose of the vessel.

“The Ark was never built merely to carry the living through the waters. It was built to reveal what the living would become when the old world could no longer sustain them.”

The three angels bowed their heads.

But Wenitte remained standing.

“Then tell us,” he asked, “what awaits us when the waters withdraw?”

The light flickered.

Beyond the observation window, the ocean began to recede.

Far away, the uppermost floors of Manhattan’s drowned towers emerged from the waves. Birds of luminous code circled the ruins, and strange green growth spread across the broken rooftops.

The Unnamed Deity answered:

“A world that remembers its destruction but is not governed by it.

“A world that recognizes danger without confusing difference with evil.

“A world in which power is measured by the life it protects, rather than the obedience it commands.”

Then the light disappeared.

For the first time in forty-one days, the Ark’s navigation system came alive.

A single destination appeared on the central display.

MANHATTAN — NEW GROUND DETECTED.

V. The Fourth Sign

As the Ark turned toward the emerging coastline, Helena noticed something unusual in the recovered archives.

A new message had appeared.

It was not written in any known language.

Parmita examined its structure and found that it was neither a command nor a warning.

Alexandra studied the signal and discovered that it originated from somewhere beneath the drowned city.

The three angels stood before Wenitte.

“There is something below Manhattan that survived the flood,” Helena said.

“Something that does not appear in the Ark’s records,” Parmita added.

Alexandra looked toward the darkening horizon.

“And something that knows we are coming.”

At that moment, every digital creature aboard the Ark turned toward the same direction.

The Glass Leviathans began to tremble.

The Null Seraphs fell silent.

Deep beneath the waves, a colossal shape opened its eyes.

And somewhere in the depths of the drowned city, a voice spoke a name that had not been heard since the beginning of the world.

“Wenitte.”

The Ark’s lights went out.

